//! THE PAIR — `PipeInlet` and `PipeOutlet`, the two ends of a connection (spec
//! §2.1; sawos design 10 D-1, built by design 13).
//!
//! **ONE FILE FOR TWO TYPES, BECAUSE THE PAIR IS ONE OBJECT.** Every other
//! module here is one file per §2 row, and this one is two rows — deliberately:
//! an inlet and an outlet are two counted KINDS over one connection, so every
//! sentence that explains either explains it by contrast with the other. Neither
//! type names the other (an inlet has no way to reach its outlet, and that is the
//! capability model working), so the module graph did not force this; readability
//! did, which is the placement rule the facade's header states now that extension
//! lookup follows `public import` (sawlang 0.2.0, SL-7 closed).
//!
//! It sits one step above the floor and names nothing else, exactly as `memory`
//! does: the FACTORY is `Process.pipe_create`, because it needs a `Process` and
//! `Process` is declared in `sos.system`, which imports this file. See there for
//! the shape that ordering forces — it is the same one `Process.map` has.
//!
//! **FOUR TYPES SINCE M4 UNIT 2** (sawos design 14), and the pair-explains-the-
//! pair reason applies twice: `PipeReply` is the CLIENT's claim on one reply and
//! `PipeRequest` is the SERVER's obligation to send it, so the file holds two
//! pairs and every sentence about either half of either pair reads by contrast
//! with its opposite. They are §2.1's ratified `PipeReplyHandle` /
//! `PipeRequestHandle` under the tree's own convention — a §2 object name renders
//! short at the typed tier, exactly as `MemoryObject` renders as `Memory`.
//!
//! **THE THREE OPS POLL, AND SINCE M4 UNIT 3 THEY ARE NOT THE ONLY DOOR** (sawos
//! design 15). A full ring, an empty one and a reply that has not been written
//! yet are all "not right now" — and at THIS tier that is not an error: `post`
//! answers `Ok(Some(claim))` or `Ok(None)`, `take` answers
//! `Ok(Some((len, request)))` or `Ok(None)`, and `resolve` answers
//! `Ok(Some(len))` or `Ok(None)`, on design 234 §4's two-channel shape, so a
//! poll loop never matches an error case for a condition that clears itself.
//! What IS an error is a peer that is gone, and it is terminal.
//!
//! What unit 3 added beside them is the PARK: all four kinds are §2.2 waitables,
//! and the eight `Waiter` overloads at the bottom of this file are how they
//! attach. A caller chooses the door by whether it wants to wait, never by what
//! it will be told.
//!
//! **THE BLOCKING SEND IS THE CALLER'S OWN COMPOSITION, and there is no wrapper
//! type for it** (user ruling, lead review Sep 1). §2.1's ratified `send(msg)`
//! and `send(msg, timeout:)` are post + attach + wait, plus a Timer on the same
//! Waiter for the timeout — four calls a client writes inline out of the
//! primitives above. A library type that froze that shape would freeze it
//! against the FUSED path (`Call`) that unit 3.5 puts in the kernel, and a
//! kernel-side timeout, if one is ever ruled in, arrives as an op rather than
//! as a method on a wrapper. `tests/pipe-send-manual` is the composition
//! written out.
//!
//! **SINGLE USE IS RAII HERE AND A LEDGER IN THE KERNEL — AND AT THE TWO REPLY
//! OPS IT IS NOW A COMPILE ERROR** (SL-16, closed against sawlang design 260 at
//! sawc 0.4.0). `reply` and `reply_wait` consume the obligation on EVERY path they
//! can take, so they are `consumes` methods: the declaration carries the word in
//! its effect slot beside `&var self`, the call site spells
//! `(move obligation).reply(...)`, and a second use through the same binding is
//! refused by the compiler rather than diagnosed by the kernel. NO SENTINEL IS
//! WRITTEN AT EITHER OP any more — a consuming body substitutes for the custom
//! `deinit` body, so the husk that used to carry `NO_HANDLE` does not exist, the
//! handle word's synthesized drop is a no-op, and no release fires.
//!
//! **`resolve` IS DELIBERATELY NOT ONE, and the reason is the documented
//! difference it always had**: a `resolve` that answers `Ok(None)` consumed
//! NOTHING. `consumes` consumes on every path — the empty poll included — so
//! spelling it that way would spend a live claim the first time the answer had not
//! arrived yet. Its contract is consume-on-success-or-terminal-failure, which the
//! effect cannot express, so it keeps its `&var self` receiver and its disarm
//! decided by the ANSWER. What stands behind it is what always did: the kernel's
//! ledger, and `pipe-dead-claim` is that fault, still reachable and still proved.
//!
//! The transfer funnels that are NOT receivers — the four `Waiter.give` overloads
//! below — keep the disarm, because `consumes` is a receiver-position effect and
//! they take their value as a by-value PARAMETER. A private `consumes` dissolve
//! helper on each wrapper would retire those too; that is SL-3's remainder and it
//! sits in the tracker's backlog, deliberately not done here.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, PipeInletHandle, PipeInletOp, PipeOutletHandle,
               PipeOutletOp, PipeReplyHandle, PipeRequestHandle, MINT_OP,
               NO_HANDLE, PIPE_BODY_BYTES, PIPE_MSG_HANDLES,
               BootHandleKind, InletLevel, OutletLevel, ReplyLevel,
               MemoryHandle, IoMemoryHandle,
               PipeMsgMeta, PipeMsgRecord, PipeSendRecord, PipeReplyWaitRecord,
               PipeTakeRecord,
               ReplyWaitLeg, WaitBuffer, WaitTag, WaiterHandle,
               WAIT_HEADER_WORDS, WAIT_KEY_WORD, WAIT_TAG_WORD,
               WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD, WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS, WAIT_REQUEST_SLOT,
               WAIT_MSG_SLOT_0,
               pipe_msg_record_bytes, pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes,
               pipe_send_record_bytes, pipe_take_record_bytes,
               wait_record_bytes}

import sos.floor.{ALL_RIGHTS, call_value, checked, polled_ok, polled_value,
                  sos_handle_release, sos_pipe_inlet_call, sos_pipe_inlet_post,
                  sos_pipe_outlet_take, sos_pipe_reply_resolve,
                  sos_pipe_request_reply, sos_pipe_request_reply_wait,
                  sos_waiter_wait}
import sos.waiter.{AttachMode, Waiter, mode_flag}
import sos.memory.{Memory}
import sos.iomemory.{IoMemory}

// =============================================================================
// THE MESSAGE VOCABULARY (spec §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)`; sawos design 18,
// amended at the STOP and user-ruled Sep 2)
// =============================================================================
//
// **IT LIVES HERE BECAUSE A MESSAGE CAN CARRY A CAPABILITY, AND A CAPABILITY AT
// THIS TIER IS AN OWNING WRAPPER.** Unit 3 declared `PipeMsg`, `WaitResult` and
// `WaitPayload` in `sosabi`, which was right for as long as a delivery was words
// and bytes: one declaration served the kernel that writes the record and the
// process that reads it. Handles broke that — `sos` depends on `sosabi` and
// nothing depends the other way, which is the whole of why op numbers can be
// kernel-internal — so the decoded forms moved to the highest module that can
// name every wrapper a message may carry, and `decode_wait` and `Waiter.wait`
// came with them.
//
// WHAT STAYED IN `sosabi` IS THE WIRE: the tag, the three level enums, the
// record layouts and their offsets. The rule is unchanged and the altitude
// moved, which is design 8's own sentence about `kcore.waitables` seen from the
// userspace side.

/// **A HANDLE THAT ARRIVED IN A MESSAGE, TYPED AT THE BOUNDARY** — the
/// `decode_boot_handle` precedent generalized (§API, user-ruled Sep 2).
///
/// **EACH CASE OWNS A WRAPPER**, which is what makes a delivered capability the
/// same kind of thing as one that arrived any other way: it is `NoCopy`, its
/// drop is a release, and the module's no-forged-handles rule holds — a sender
/// builds one by MOVING a wrapper in and a receiver matches it out.
///
/// **SIX CASES, AND THE SET IS "GIVABLE AND DECLARABLE"** (user-ruled at the
/// STOP). Givable, because staging validates the universal `Transfer` bit and
/// only six kinds mint it: a Thread, Event, Waiter, Interrupt, Clock or Timer
/// handle cannot be attached at all, so a case for one would be dead surface on
/// the rule that has `ClockType` declare one domain. Declarable, because a
/// `Process` and a `System` are declared in `sos.system`, which imports THIS
/// module — a case for either would be the import cycle DF-232e does not
/// diagnose. The kernel refuses to stage those two rather than delivering
/// something the receiver could not be told the name of; see `msg_kind_of`.
/// Lifting that is a NAMED FOLLOW-UP and it wants a ruling, not a workaround.
///
/// **TAKE IT OUT OF ITS SLOT BEFORE YOU MATCH IT.** `msg.handles[i].take()`
/// hands over an owned local, and a `match` on an owned local CONSUMES it, which
/// is what lets an arm move the wrapper into a consuming op. Matching the SLOT
/// in place is a borrow, and a `move` out of a borrowing arm is a sawc soundness
/// hole this unit met and filed (SL-17) — so the idiom is take-then-match, which
/// is the shape that reads best anyway.
public enum PipeHandle {
    case Memory(m: Memory),
    case IoMemory(io: IoMemory),
    case Inlet(i: PipeInlet),
    case Outlet(o: PipeOutlet),
    case Reply(r: PipeReply),
    case Request(rq: PipeRequest),
}

extension PipeHandle: NoCopy {}

/// **THE HANDLES A MESSAGE IS BEING SENT WITH** — the send side's owning
/// carrier (sawos design 18).
///
/// **IT IS NOT `PipeHandle`, AND THE REASON IS A LANGUAGE FACT.** A wrapper held
/// inside an enum payload can never be DISARMED: a match-arm binding is
/// immutable under every spelling (probed — `&var self` included), so there is
/// no way to write the transfer-funnel contract `Process.give` runs on. So the
/// send side takes the wrapper at the DOOR, disarms it there, and keeps the kind
/// and the word — which is exactly what `Process.give`'s three funnels do, one
/// overload per givable kind.
///
/// **IT IS `NoCopy` AND ITS DROP RELEASES WHAT IT STILL HOLDS**, which is what
/// makes every path honest. A carrier that is built and never posted releases
/// its handles, exactly as the wrappers would have; a post that SUCCEEDS clears
/// the slots, so the entries stay bound for the rendezvous and the drop is a
/// no-op; and a post that is REFUSED leaves the slots alone, so the caller may
/// retry with the very same carrier or drop it. That is the property a poll
/// loop needs: `Ok(None)` must not cost a caller its capabilities.
public struct PipeHandles {
    public(package) kinds: [UInt8; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
    public(package) words: [UInt; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
}

extension PipeHandles: NoCopy {
    /// Release whatever is still here. A posted carrier is empty by then, which
    /// is what makes this the un-posted path's cleanup and nothing else.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        var i: Int = 0
        while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
            if self.words[i] != NO_HANDLE {
                let _ = sos_handle_release(self.words[i])
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
}

extension PipeHandles {
    /// A carrier with every slot empty — what a message with no handles sends,
    /// and the default every sending method takes.
    public static func none() -> PipeHandles {
        PipeHandles(kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
                    words: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES])
    }

    /// How many slots there are. Published so a caller can loop rather than
    /// spell `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES` (which it may, it is on the facade).
    public func slots(&self) -> Int { PIPE_MSG_HANDLES }

    /// **PUT A HANDLE IN SLOT `at`, CONSUMING THE WRAPPER** — one overload per
    /// givable kind, which is `Process.give`'s own shape and for its own reason:
    /// the funnel reads the wrapper's handle field and disarms it, and that is
    /// only writable where the wrapper is a struct rather than an enum payload.
    ///
    /// An occupied slot is OVERWRITTEN and the handle that was there is released
    /// — the same thing that would have happened to a wrapper the caller
    /// dropped, said at the one place that can say it. `at` outside the slot
    /// range PANICS, on the house accessor rule (design 130): the count is
    /// published, so the caller could have checked.
    public func put(&var self, at: Int, memory: Memory) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move memory
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.Memory as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = MemoryHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, iomemory: IoMemory) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move iomemory
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.IoMemory as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = IoMemoryHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, inlet: PipeInlet) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move inlet
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.PipeInlet as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeInletHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, outlet: PipeOutlet) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move outlet
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.PipeOutlet as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeOutletHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, reply: PipeReply) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move reply
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.PipeReply as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeReplyHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, request: PipeRequest) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move request
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.PipeRequest as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeRequestHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    /// Empty slot `at`, releasing whatever was in it. The bounds check lives
    /// here, so the six funnels above read as one line of intent each.
    func free_slot(&var self, at: Int) {
        if at < 0 || at >= PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
            panic("PipeHandles.put: slot out of range: {} (slots {})", at,
                  PIPE_MSG_HANDLES)
        }
        if self.words[at] != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.words[at])
            self.words[at] = NO_HANDLE
            self.kinds[at] = 0
        }
    }

    /// Forget every slot WITHOUT releasing — what a successful send calls, once
    /// the kernel has taken the words. The entries stay bound in this process's
    /// table until the rendezvous moves them, which is `designs/010` ruling 5.
    func handed_over(&var self) {
        var i: Int = 0
        while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
            self.kinds[i] = 0
            self.words[i] = NO_HANDLE
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
}

/// A message: how many of its bytes are real, the handles it carried, and the
/// bytes (sawos design 15, GROWN by design 18 — §API, user-ruled Sep 2).
///
/// **HANDLES BEFORE BYTES, and the Saw struct mirrors the record's order**
/// (declaration-order ABI, design 58). It is the same layout the kernel writes,
/// read from the other side.
///
/// **`NoCopy`, AND NOW FOR TWO REASONS.** The bytes were always API discipline —
/// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` of inline storage should not be duplicated by an accident
/// of binding — and the handles are ownership proper: each is a capability this
/// process holds exactly once.
///
/// Take a handle out with `msg.handles[i].take()`, which needs a `var` binding
/// of the message and hands back an owned `PipeHandle?`. A slot the sender left
/// empty, and one whose sender released its entry before the rendezvous, are
/// both `None` — `designs/010` ruling 5's honest absence.
public struct PipeMsg {
    public len: UInt,
    public handles: [PipeHandle?; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
    public bytes: [UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
}

extension PipeMsg: NoCopy {}

/// What became ready, decoded — one case per waitable kind, plus the one M4
/// unit 4 added.
///
/// **IT CARRIES NO OWNED VALUE ANY MORE, AND THAT IS FORCED** (sawos design 18).
/// Unit 3's `Reply` case carried the reply's bytes inside the payload, which
/// worked because bytes are read through a borrow; a delivery carries HANDLES
/// now, and a handle has to be MOVED out to be used. A `move` out of a
/// place-match arm is a sawc soundness hole (SL-17 — it compiles and drops the
/// value twice), so an owned delivery cannot live inside this enum at all. It
/// lives in `WaitResult`'s own fields, where a `.take()` reaches it, and what
/// stays here is the LEVEL — which is what a tag-shaped enum should have been
/// carrying all along.
public enum WaitPayload {
    case Event(word: UInt),
    case Interrupt(line: UInt),
    case Timer(fires: UInt),
    case Process(status: UInt),
    /// The SERVER end has something to take, or will never have anything again.
    case Readable(state: OutletLevel),
    /// The CLIENT end has room to post, or nobody is left to take.
    case Room(state: InletLevel),
    /// The answer to one exchange arrived, or never will. `Data` means
    /// `WaitResult.message` is populated.
    case Reply(state: ReplyLevel),
    /// The claim behind this obligation was dropped. There is nothing to say
    /// beyond the fact, which is why this case has no payload.
    case Abandoned,
    /// **THE DELIVERY WAS THE TAKE** (`designs/010` ruling 11(d)) — a
    /// persistent-consuming outlet attachment handing over a message and the
    /// obligation to answer it. `WaitResult.message` and `WaitResult.request`
    /// are both populated, and the server never called `take` at all.
    case Message,
}

/// `Waiter.wait`'s answer: the attacher's key, what fired, and whatever the
/// delivery HANDED OVER.
///
/// **THE TWO OWNING FIELDS ARE M4 UNIT 4's** (sawos design 18). They are
/// `Optional`s rather than payloads for the reason `WaitPayload`'s docstring
/// gives — a `.take()` reaches a field and cannot reach a match arm — and they
/// are the reason this type is `NoCopy`. Which of them is populated is decided
/// by `what` and never has to be guessed:
///
///   `Reply(Data)`  -> `message`
///   `Message`      -> `message` AND `request`
///   anything else  -> neither
public struct WaitResult {
    /// The word supplied at `add`, handed back unread.
    public key: UInt,
    public what: WaitPayload,
    /// The message a `Reply(Data)` or a `Message` delivery carried.
    public message: PipeMsg?,
    /// The obligation a `Message` delivery minted into this process's table.
    public request: PipeRequest?,
}

extension WaitResult: NoCopy {}

/// One handle slot of a delivered record, typed.
///
/// AN UNKNOWN KIND PANICS, on `decode_wait`'s exact terms: the KERNEL wrote the
/// byte out of the same `BootHandleKind` declaration this function reads, so a
/// value that does not decode is a kernel/`sos` skew rather than data from an
/// untrusted source. `Process` and `System` panic for a sharper reason — the
/// kernel refuses to stage either, so a message carrying one means the two
/// halves disagree about which kinds a message may carry.
func decode_handle(kind: UInt8, word: UInt) -> PipeHandle? {
    if word == NO_HANDLE {
        return None
    }
    guard let what = BootHandleKind.from(raw: kind as UInt) else {
        panic("sos: kernel wrote message handle kind {}, which this build cannot name",
              kind as UInt)
    }
    match what {
        case Memory -> PipeHandle.Memory(m: Memory(handle: MemoryHandle(word))),
        case IoMemory -> PipeHandle.IoMemory(io: IoMemory(handle: IoMemoryHandle(word))),
        case PipeInlet -> PipeHandle.Inlet(i: PipeInlet(handle: PipeInletHandle(word))),
        case PipeOutlet -> PipeHandle.Outlet(o: PipeOutlet(handle: PipeOutletHandle(word))),
        case PipeReply -> PipeHandle.Reply(r: PipeReply(handle: PipeReplyHandle(word))),
        case PipeRequest -> PipeHandle.Request(rq: PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(word))),
        case Process -> {
            panic("sos: a message carried a Process handle, which the kernel refuses to stage")
        },
        case System -> {
            panic("sos: a message carried a System handle, which the kernel refuses to stage")
        },
    }
}

/// Build a `PipeMsg` out of a delivered record's meta, handle words and bytes.
/// The one place a message crosses from words into wrappers.
func msg_of(meta: &PipeMsgMeta, words: &[UInt; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
            bytes: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]) -> PipeMsg {
    var msg = PipeMsg(len: meta.len as UInt,
                      handles: [None, None, None, None],
                      bytes: bytes)
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
        msg.handles[i] = decode_handle(meta.kinds[i], words[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    move msg
}

// `msg_of` spells its empty-slot array out element by element, because a REPEAT
// literal cannot build a NoCopy optional — `[v; N]` needs an element that copies
// for free and a `PipeHandle?` is move-only. So that one literal tracks this
// number by hand, and this is what catches it if the number moves.
static_assert(PIPE_MSG_HANDLES == 4,
              "msg_of's empty-slot literal is spelled out and tracks PIPE_MSG_HANDLES by hand")

/// Decode a record the kernel copied out into `WaitResult`.
///
/// AN UNKNOWN TAG PANICS, loudly, and that is deliberate. Every other decode in
/// this package treats its input as data a process might legitimately be handed;
/// this one does not, because the KERNEL wrote it out of the same `WaitTag`
/// declaration this function reads. A tag that does not decode is therefore a
/// skew between the kernel and the `sos` module it exports — a bug in the pair,
/// not a runtime condition — and the never-hide rule says a bug the caller
/// cannot act on should stop the program where it happened rather than become
/// an `Err` somebody maps to "unknown".
///
/// **THE SAME RULE COVERS THE PAYLOAD'S OWN SUB-ENUMS** (sawos design 15) and
/// the handle kinds beside them (design 18). All of them arrive out of
/// declarations the kernel wrote from.
///
/// `public(package)` since M4 unit 3.5 (sawos design 17): `PipeRequest.reply_wait`
/// is a SECOND door onto the same delivery. One record, one decode — a second
/// copy of this match would be the one place a kernel/`sos` skew could hide.
public(package) func decode_wait(buf: &WaitBuffer) -> WaitResult {
    let key = buf.header[WAIT_KEY_WORD]
    let tag = buf.header[WAIT_TAG_WORD]
    let payload = buf.header[WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD]
    guard let kind = WaitTag.from(raw: tag) else {
        panic("sos: kernel wrote wait tag {}, which this build cannot name", tag)
    }
    match kind {
        case Event -> WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Event(word: payload),
                                 message: None, request: None),
        case Interrupt -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                     what: WaitPayload.Interrupt(line: payload),
                                     message: None, request: None),
        case Timer -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                 what: WaitPayload.Timer(fires: payload),
                                 message: None, request: None),
        // THE FOURTH TAG (sawos design 8, M3 unit 5.5): a process ended, and
        // the payload is §8's status word — the same bits `Process.get_status`
        // answers with, so a supervisor parses one thing however it learned.
        case Process -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                   what: WaitPayload.Process(status: payload),
                                   message: None, request: None),
        // **THE FOUR PIPE TAGS ARE M4 UNIT 3's** (sawos design 15) and the fifth
        // is unit 4's.
        case Readable -> {
            guard let level = OutletLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote outlet level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Readable(state: level),
                       message: None, request: None)
        },
        case Room -> {
            guard let level = InletLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote inlet level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Room(state: level),
                       message: None, request: None)
        },
        case Reply -> {
            guard let level = ReplyLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote reply level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            var got: PipeMsg? = None
            match level {
                case Data -> {
                    got = wait_message(&buf)
                },
                case PeerClosed -> {},
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Reply(state: level),
                       message: move got, request: None)
        },
        // NOTHING TO DECODE, and that is the case rather than an omission: the
        // fact IS the notification, so the payload word carries no information
        // and `WaitPayload.Abandoned` binds nothing.
        case Abandoned -> WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Abandoned,
                                     message: None, request: None),
        // **RULING 11(d)'s DELIVERY-AS-TAKE.** The record carries the message
        // AND the obligation the kernel minted at the delivery, so the server
        // never reaches for `take` at all.
        case Message -> {
            let word = buf.handles[WAIT_REQUEST_SLOT]
            var obligation: PipeRequest? = None
            if word != NO_HANDLE {
                obligation = PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(word))
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Message,
                       message: wait_message(&buf), request: move obligation)
        },
    }
}

/// The message half of a delivered wait record. Split out because two tags reach
/// it and the handle region is at one offset for both — which is what the
/// fixed-prefix rule bought.
func wait_message(buf: &WaitBuffer) -> PipeMsg? {
    var words: [UInt; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES] = [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
        words[i] = buf.handles[WAIT_MSG_SLOT_0 + i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    let got: PipeMsg? = msg_of(&buf.meta, &words, &buf.body)
    move got
}

extension Waiter {
    /// Block until an attached handle is ready, and answer with the attacher's
    /// key and what fired. Requires `WaiterRight.Wait`.
    ///
    /// **IT IS DECLARED HERE RATHER THAN IN `sos.waiter`** (sawos design 18),
    /// and the reason is its RETURN TYPE: a delivery may hand over a message
    /// full of capabilities, so `WaitResult` names every wrapper a message can
    /// carry and therefore sits in this module. `Waiter.add(outlet:)` and
    /// `Waiter.add(process:)` are the same shape for the same kind of reason,
    /// and the facade's `public import` makes all of it one list at the call
    /// site.
    ///
    /// Returns IMMEDIATELY when something is already ready, which is what
    /// level-triggering buys: the readiness a signal left behind is still
    /// there, so "signal then wait" and "wait then signal" have the same
    /// outcome and only the second one parks.
    ///
    /// THE ANSWER CONSUMES WHAT IT REPORTS, for an Event and a Timer alike: the
    /// record carries the accumulated word (or the fire count) and the object is
    /// emptied in the same step, so nothing is reported twice. An Interrupt is
    /// the exception and §9 is why — its readiness ends at `ack`.
    ///
    /// SEVERAL THREADS ON ONE WAITER IS A DISTRIBUTION: a ready handle wakes one
    /// of them and that thread's record carries the value, so a worker pool
    /// shares a stream of notifications with no coordination.
    ///
    /// **A PARK CAN END IN `Err(SosStatus.Revoked)`** (sawos design 12, M4 unit
    /// 0): if this Waiter's last handle is released while you are parked on it,
    /// the kernel WAKES you with that status rather than leaving you blocked
    /// forever. No record is written on that path.
    ///
    /// **AND IN `Err(NoResource)` / `Err(QuotaExceeded)` SINCE M4 UNIT 4**
    /// (`designs/010` ruling 5's atomic refusal). A delivery that hands over
    /// handles has to mint them into this process's table, and a full table
    /// delivers NOTHING — the message stays staged, its level stays raised, and
    /// waiting again after freeing a slot is handed the very same message. It is
    /// a resource condition rather than a bug, which is why it is a status.
    ///
    /// THE BUFFER IS THIS FRAME'S, and a caller never sees it. **IT IS
    /// MAX-SIZED AND THE COPY IS NOT** (`designs/010` ruling 11(a)): a parked
    /// waiter cannot predict which arm fires, so the buffer holds the biggest
    /// answer its attachments could give, while the kernel writes only what the
    /// delivery needs.
    public func wait(&self) -> Result<WaitResult, SosStatus> {
        wait_into_frame(self.handle)
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `wait`, confined to one small named helper (design 136).
/// The buffer's address never leaves it.
func wait_into_frame(handle: WaiterHandle) unsafe -> Result<WaitResult, SosStatus> {
    var buf = WaitBuffer(header: [0; WAIT_HEADER_WORDS],
                         meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0, kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                         handles: [NO_HANDLE; WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS],
                         body: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    try checked(sos_waiter_wait(handle,
                                (&var buf.header) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                wait_record_bytes() as UInt))
    return decode_wait(&buf)
}

/// A handle to a PipeInlet — the CLIENT end of a connection, where requests flow
/// IN (spec §2.1; the names are design 10 ruling 2's).
///
/// **A ROLE IS AN OBJECT, NOT A RIGHTS MASK** (design 10 D-1). The kernel does
/// not carry one symmetric pipe object with read and write rights on it, because
/// a parked receiver is itself a holder and a write-capable count on such a thing
/// could never reach zero — the socket-`shutdown()` hole. The bidirectionality
/// dissolves instead: holding one of these means you are the client, and "the
/// writers went to zero" is "every PipeInlet handle onto this connection is
/// gone", which the ordinary reference ledger answers.
///
/// Built by `Process.pipe_create` and by `BootHandle.take_inlet`, and by nothing
/// else — no word constructor, no word extractor, the module docstring's rule.
///
/// **`NoCopy`, AND DROPPING IT RELEASES THE HANDLE** (sawos design 3 D-5). What
/// makes that interesting here rather than merely tidy: dropping the LAST inlet
/// is what tells the far end that no request will ever arrive again. Release is
/// the only way to say it, and RAII is what makes saying it the default —
/// `let _ = move inlet` is a deliberate close, and letting one fall out of scope
/// is the same act written by the language.
public struct PipeInlet {
    public(package) handle: PipeInletHandle,
}

extension PipeInlet: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension PipeInlet {
    /// **STAGE A MESSAGE FOR THE FAR END.** Requires `PipeInletRight.Post`.
    ///
    /// `Ok(Some(claim))` the message is staged; `Ok(None)` THE RING IS FULL and
    /// nothing was staged — call again once an exchange has settled. The absence
    /// is not a failure and does not belong in the error channel: design 234 §4's
    /// rule, that a poll loop must be able to tell "not yet" from "broken", read
    /// at the sending end.
    ///
    /// **IT NEVER BLOCKS AND NEVER WILL** (design 10 ruling 4): `post` is the
    /// ONE kernel primitive on the client side, and blocking send,
    /// `send(timeout:)`, TELL and cancel are all compositions over it plus the
    /// Timer and Waiter machinery — which is what keeps the kernel from ever
    /// learning what a timeout is. **THE PIECES THEY COMPOSE OUT OF ARE ALL
    /// HERE SINCE M4 UNIT 3** (sawos design 15) — post, the four attaches, the
    /// wait — and the composition itself is the CALLER's, written inline (user
    /// ruling, lead review Sep 1: no wrapper type, so nothing freezes a shape
    /// the fused `Call` will want to replace).
    ///
    /// `len` may be ZERO — a data-free message is §2.1's own option — and a
    /// zero-length post reads no buffer at all. A `len` above `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`
    /// TERMINATES the caller: the maximum is published, and the caller chose the
    /// number.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` means every outlet handle onto this
    /// connection is gone, so nobody can ever take what you would send. It is
    /// TERMINAL — nothing un-closes — so a caller may stop rather than retry.
    ///
    /// **AND IT ANSWERS THE CLAIM** (sawos design 14; §2.1's `send` yields a
    /// `PipeReplyHandle`, and this is it). `Ok(Some(claim))` is a staged message
    /// AND the client's claim on the one reply it is owed; hold it and `resolve`
    /// it, hand it on with `give`, or DROP it — dropping is §2.1's cancellation
    /// primitive and the TELL idiom's second half, and the server is told by its
    /// `reply` answering `PeerClosed`.
    ///
    /// **A CLAIM HOLDS A RING SLOT UNTIL THE EXCHANGE SETTLES**, which is what
    /// makes `Ok(None)` mean more than it did in unit 1: a full ring counts
    /// AWAITING-REPLY exchanges now, not merely untaken messages, so a client
    /// that never resolves will meet its own in-flight budget.
    public func post(&self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                     len: UInt) -> Result<PipeReply?, SosStatus> {
        var none = PipeHandles.none()
        return post_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var none)
    }

    /// **THE SAME POST, CARRYING HANDLES** (spec §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)`;
    /// `designs/010` ruling 5, built by sawos design 18).
    ///
    /// **IT IS A SECOND NAME RATHER THAN A SECOND OVERLOAD, AND SL-15 IS WHY.**
    /// A bare integer literal stops adopting a `UInt` parameter the moment a
    /// method has two candidates, so `post(body:, len: 1)` would have needed an
    /// `as UInt` at every one of the thirty-eight call sites in this tree. The
    /// four `_with` verbs are the workaround, and they collapse into overloads
    /// with no call-site change on the day that deficiency closes.
    ///
    /// **A SECOND OVERLOAD RATHER THAN A DEFAULTED PARAMETER**, and the carrier
    /// is by `&var` rather than by value, because a refused post MUST NOT COST A
    /// CALLER ITS CAPABILITIES: `Ok(None)` is a full ring and the ordinary thing
    /// a poll loop meets, so the handles have to still be there to try again
    /// with. On the staged path the carrier is emptied — the kernel holds the
    /// words now — and on every refusal it is untouched.
    ///
    /// **THE ENTRIES STAY YOURS UNTIL THE RENDEZVOUS** (ruling 5), which has one
    /// consequence worth reading: releasing one afterwards is legal and makes
    /// that slot arrive EMPTY at the receiver. Fire-and-forget is a shape §2.1
    /// blesses, so the ledger never has an in-flight limbo state.
    public func post_with(&self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                          handles: &var PipeHandles) -> Result<PipeReply?, SosStatus> {
        post_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var handles)
    }

    /// **SEND A MESSAGE AND WAIT FOR ITS ANSWER — one trap, and §2.1's ratified
    /// `send` arriving at last** (sawos design 17, on design 10 ruling 4's
    /// Aug-31 rider). Requires `PipeInletRight.Post`, the same bit `post`
    /// spends.
    ///
    /// **IT IS THE FUSED `Call`, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A METHOD AT ALL.** Unit
    /// 3 landed a library `send` written over post + attach + wait and the user
    /// struck it at review, for a reason this method is the other half of: a
    /// wrapper TYPE freezes a shape that cannot later be refactored into a
    /// simple kernel call. This one IS the simple kernel call — one op, no
    /// Waiter, no attachment, no claim handle — so there is nothing to freeze
    /// and nothing to refactor into later.
    ///
    /// **NOTHING IS MINTED, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** A fused call's claim is
    /// kernel-internal and tied to this thread, so a blocking client holds no
    /// `PipeReply` at all: no table row, no quota, no RAII to get right. Design
    /// 14 finding 5 named this as the answer for a client whose `MAX_HANDLES`
    /// bounded its in-flight depth before its ring did.
    ///
    /// **IT BLOCKS, AND A FULL RING BLOCKS TOO** — never `WouldBlock`. The
    /// caller parks on the connection's room-to-post level and resumes when an
    /// exchange settles, so backpressure is a wait rather than a poll loop
    /// (`designs/010` ruling 8, reached from inside the op).
    ///
    /// **THERE IS NO TIMEOUT ARGUMENT AND THERE WILL NOT BE ONE HERE** (ruling
    /// 4): the kernel does not know what a timeout is. A caller that needs a
    /// deadline composes `post` + `add(reply:)` + a Timer on one Waiter, which
    /// is what `tests/pipe-send-manual` writes out.
    ///
    /// `len` may be ZERO — a data-free message is §2.1's own option. A `len`
    /// above `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` TERMINATES the caller, exactly as at a post.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` is `resolve`'s own convention and covers
    /// every way an answer can fail to arrive: the server end was already gone,
    /// the server dropped the obligation, or the server end went while this
    /// thread was parked. It is terminal for THIS exchange; whether the
    /// connection has a future is what a later call finds out.
    public func send(&self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                     len: UInt) -> Result<PipeMsg, SosStatus> {
        var none = PipeHandles.none()
        return call_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var none)
    }

    /// **THE SAME CALL, CARRYING HANDLES** — everything `post(handles:)` says
    /// about the carrier holds, with one difference the fused path makes: a
    /// `Call` never answers a transient refusal, so the carrier is emptied on
    /// every path but a fault. And the ANSWER may carry handles too, because
    /// §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)` is symmetric — the `PipeMsg` that comes
    /// back has slots of its own.
    public func send_with(&self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                          handles: &var PipeHandles) -> Result<PipeMsg, SosStatus> {
        call_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var handles)
    }

    /// **MINT A SIBLING of this inlet, narrowed by a KEEP MASK** — the universal
    /// `MINT_OP`, on `Memory.mint`'s exact terms. Requires the universal
    /// `PipeInletRight.Mint` on THIS handle.
    ///
    /// **THIS IS WHERE A LAUNCHER'S PIPE POLICY IS WRITTEN.** An endpoint's
    /// default set is permissive — `Transfer | Mint | Post`, and it has to be,
    /// because attenuation is monotonic and a bit not minted at creation could
    /// never appear later — so what a child may do with the end it is handed is
    /// decided here, once, by the mask:
    ///
    /// ```saw
    /// let for_child = inlet.mint(rights: PipeInletRight.Transfer
    ///                                    | PipeInletRight.Post)!
    /// child.give(inlet: move for_child, tag: REQUESTS)
    /// ```
    ///
    /// That child can post and can hand the end on, and can never mint a
    /// narrower sibling of its own.
    ///
    /// **A SIBLING IS A SECOND NAME AND THEREFORE A SECOND REFERENCE** on this
    /// connection's INLET column, which is what makes D-1's v1 rule — any handle
    /// to an end holds that end open, the Unix `dup` precedent — true by
    /// construction. Dropping one of two siblings tells the far end nothing;
    /// only the last one going is news.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<PipeInlet, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return PipeInlet(handle: PipeInletHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeInlet.post`, confined to one small named helper
/// (design 136). The body's address never leaves it.
///
/// The word the kernel answers with is a HANDLE, and this is the one place it is
/// ever seen as a number: the wrapper is built here and everything above this
/// line owns a `PipeReply` instead.
func post_body(handle: PipeInletHandle, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
               len: UInt,
               handles: &var PipeHandles) unsafe -> Result<PipeReply?, SosStatus> {
    var args = send_record(&body, len, &handles)
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_pipe_inlet_post(handle,
                                  (&var args) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                  pipe_send_record_bytes() as UInt,
                                  (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    let word = try polled_value(raw, value)
    guard let staged = word else { return None }
    // **THE CARRIER IS EMPTIED ONLY ON THE STAGED PATH.** The kernel holds the
    // words now and the entries stay bound until the rendezvous moves them, so
    // a release here would deliver every slot as `None`; a refusal never
    // reaches this line, so the caller keeps everything it had.
    handles.handed_over()
    let claim: PipeReply? = PipeReply(handle: PipeReplyHandle(staged))
    return move claim
}

/// Assemble the argument record every sending op takes — a body address, a
/// length and the handle words (sawos design 18).
///
/// **IT READS THE CARRIER AND DOES NOT EMPTY IT**, which is what lets the three
/// callers decide per answer whether the handles were handed over. The kinds are
/// NOT sent: the kernel reads each word out of this process's own table, so it
/// knows the kind better than the caller could tell it, and a kind argument
/// would be a second description of one fact.
func send_record(body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                 handles: &PipeHandles) unsafe -> PipeSendRecord {
    var args = PipeSendRecord(body: ((&body) as UnsafePointer<UInt8>) as UInt,
                              len: len,
                              handles: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES])
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
        args.handles[i] = handles.words[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    args
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeInlet.send`, confined to one small named helper
/// (design 136). Neither address leaves it.
///
/// **THE REPLY BUFFER IS THIS FRAME'S**, exactly as `Waiter.wait`'s record
/// buffer is and for the same reason: the kernel answers into the caller's own
/// memory, so somebody has to supply the bytes, and supplying them here is what
/// keeps a raw address out of the typed surface. It is a whole slot wide
/// because a caller cannot size a buffer against a length it has not been told
/// yet — the published-minimum rule, one more time.
///
/// There is no `polled_value` here and there could not be: a fused call has no
/// would-block outcome to keep out of the error channel, which is the whole
/// difference between this and `post` beside it.
func call_body(handle: PipeInletHandle, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
               len: UInt,
               handles: &var PipeHandles) unsafe -> Result<PipeMsg, SosStatus> {
    var args = send_record(&body, len, &handles)
    var reply = PipeMsgRecord(meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0,
                                                kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                              handles: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
                              bytes: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_pipe_inlet_call(handle,
                                  (&var args) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                  pipe_send_record_bytes() as UInt,
                                  (&var reply) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                  (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    // THE CARRIER IS EMPTIED BEFORE THE ANSWER IS READ, and that is right for
    // this op and wrong for `post`: a `Call` that reaches the kernel STAGES —
    // it parks rather than refusing — so the only paths that do not are faults,
    // which never come back.
    handles.handed_over()
    try checked(raw)
    return msg_of(&reply.meta, &reply.handles, &reply.bytes)
}

/// A handle to a PipeOutlet — the SERVER end of a connection, where requests
/// come OUT (spec §2.1; design 10 ruling 2).
///
/// The mirror of `PipeInlet` in every respect but two: which reference column it
/// counts on, and which op it carries. Everything that type's docstring says
/// about roles-as-objects, about `NoCopy` and about what dropping the last one
/// means holds here with the direction reversed — a dropped last OUTLET tells
/// the client that nothing it sends can ever be read, which is the same peer-gone
/// doctrine seen from the other side.
///
/// Built by `Process.pipe_create` and by `BootHandle.take_outlet`, and by
/// nothing else.
public struct PipeOutlet {
    public(package) handle: PipeOutletHandle,
}

extension PipeOutlet: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension PipeOutlet {
    /// **TAKE THE OLDEST STAGED MESSAGE** into `into`, and answer how many bytes
    /// of it are real **AND THE OBLIGATION TO REPLY TO IT**. Requires
    /// `PipeOutletRight.Take`.
    ///
    /// `Ok(Some((n, request)))` a message arrived, its first `n` bytes are yours,
    /// and `request` is the server's obligation to send exactly one reply;
    /// `Ok(None)` NOTHING IS STAGED — call again. The `None` is design 234 §4's
    /// two-channel shape, and the reason it is not an error is that reason
    /// exactly: a server polling an idle connection is not a server meeting a
    /// failure, and a shape that could not tell those apart would make the poll
    /// loop unwritable.
    ///
    /// **THE OBLIGATION IS THE HALF M4 UNIT 2 ADDED** (sawos design 14): discharge
    /// it with `reply`, hand it on with `give` — §2.1's delegation primitive, and
    /// the reply then goes STRAIGHT to the original client — or drop it, which
    /// tells the client `PeerClosed` at its next `resolve`. Dropping is a
    /// legitimate answer; what is not available is forgetting it, because the ring
    /// slot behind it is held until the exchange settles.
    ///
    /// `n` MAY BE ZERO, and that is a message rather than an absence — §2.1
    /// makes a data-free message an option, so the two are genuinely different
    /// answers and the `Optional` is what keeps them apart.
    ///
    /// **THE BUFFER IS A WHOLE SLOT, NOT A MESSAGE.** It is
    /// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` wide because a caller cannot size a buffer against a
    /// length it has not been told yet, which is the same published-minimum rule
    /// `Waiter.wait`'s record follows.
    ///
    /// **IT NEVER PARKS**, and it does not have to since M4 unit 3: an outlet is
    /// a §2.2 waitable now, so a server attaches it (`Waiter.add(outlet:)`),
    /// waits for the readable level, and calls this once it knows there is
    /// something — which is a blocking receive composed out of two shipped ops.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` means the ring is EMPTY *and* every inlet
    /// handle is gone. **THE ORDER IS THE CONTRACT AND IT IS DRAIN-FIRST**
    /// (design 230's close-drains-first rule): everything the departed client
    /// staged is handed over before this is ever answered, so a sender exiting
    /// mid-conversation loses nothing. Afterwards it is TERMINAL — no new sender
    /// can appear, because attenuation is monotonic and a column at zero has no
    /// handle left to mint from — so every later take answers the same.
    public func take(&self) -> Result<(PipeMsg, PipeRequest)?, SosStatus> {
        take_into_frame(self.handle)
    }

    /// **MINT A SIBLING of this outlet, narrowed by a KEEP MASK.** Everything
    /// `PipeInlet.mint` says holds here with `Take` in `Post`'s place — including
    /// the part that matters for the connection: a sibling is a second reference
    /// on the OUTLET column, so a server that mints one and hands it to a helper
    /// has two names on one end and the client is told nothing until both are
    /// gone.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<PipeOutlet, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return PipeOutlet(handle: PipeOutletHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeOutlet.take`, confined to one small named helper
/// (design 136). Neither address leaves it.
///
/// **THE TUPLE IS THE ANSWER, on `Process.pipe_create`'s own reasoning** (design
/// 13 finding 4): a take that succeeds always carries EXACTLY TWO things and the
/// caller always wants both, so a destructuring `if let (n, request) = got` moves
/// each into its own binding with no husk left behind — where a `BootHandle`
/// needed an `Optional` per slot because it carries at most one of several kinds
/// and asking the wrong question must be harmless.
func take_into_frame(
    handle: PipeOutletHandle
) unsafe -> Result<(PipeMsg, PipeRequest)?, SosStatus> {
    var rec = PipeTakeRecord(
        request: NO_HANDLE,
        msg: PipeMsgRecord(meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0,
                                             kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                           handles: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
                           bytes: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]))
    let raw = sos_pipe_outlet_take(handle,
                                   (&var rec) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                   pipe_take_record_bytes() as UInt)
    let staged = try polled_ok(raw)
    if not staged {
        return None
    }
    let request = PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(rec.request))
    let got: (PipeMsg, PipeRequest)? = (msg_of(&rec.msg.meta, &rec.msg.handles,
                                               &rec.msg.bytes),
                                        move request)
    return move got
}

/// A handle to a PipeReply — the CLIENT's claim on ONE reply (spec §2.1's
/// ratified `PipeReplyHandle`; sawos design 14).
///
/// **IT IS WHAT A POST ANSWERS WITH**, and holding it is what makes
/// request/reply a PRIMITIVE rather than a convention: the reply capability is
/// unforgeable, single-use, and cannot be confused with any other exchange on the
/// same connection, because it names a RING SLOT rather than a pipe.
///
/// **`NoCopy`, AND DROPPING IT IS THE CANCELLATION PRIMITIVE** (§2.1). There is
/// no `cancel()` verb: a client that has given up releases the claim, and the
/// server learns of it when its own `reply` answers `PeerClosed`. Abandonment is
/// INFORMATION, NOT AN IMPERATIVE — whether it cancels anything is the protocol's
/// decision, and a tell-style message ignores the signal entirely. Deterministic
/// destruction is what makes the timing of the signal deterministic too.
///
/// Built by `PipeInlet.post`, by `PipeReply.mint` and by `BootHandle.take_reply`,
/// and by nothing else — no word constructor, no word extractor.
public struct PipeReply {
    public(package) handle: PipeReplyHandle,
}

extension PipeReply: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    /// A claim that has already been resolved holds the sentinel, so this is a
    /// no-op on it — single use, spelled in the value rather than checked.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension PipeReply {
    /// **TAKE THE REPLY, IF IT HAS BEEN WRITTEN.** Requires
    /// `PipeReplyRight.Resolve`.
    ///
    /// `Ok(Some(n))` the reply is here and its first `n` bytes are yours — and
    /// THE CLAIM IS SPENT: this value disarms itself, so dropping it does nothing
    /// and calling `resolve` again is the ordinary `BadHandle` fault. `Ok(None)`
    /// the exchange is still pending — nobody has replied yet, the claim is
    /// untouched, ask again. `n` may be ZERO, which is a reply that carries no
    /// data rather than an absence.
    ///
    /// **IT NEVER PARKS, AND SINCE M4 UNIT 3 IT IS THE POLLING DOOR RATHER THAN
    /// THE ONLY ONE.** The blocking `send(msg)` and `send(msg, timeout:)` §2.1
    /// ratifies are LIBRARY compositions over post + attach + wait (design 10
    /// ruling 4, and ruling 11(a) deleted the resolve leg — the answer arrives
    /// IN the wait record), and every piece they need is shipped — the
    /// composition is the caller's own four calls. A claim whose answer was
    /// delivered through a wait answers `PeerClosed` here: there is no second
    /// copy of an answer.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` means the server side abandoned it — the
    /// obligation was dropped without a reply, or the connection's server end went
    /// away before the message could ever be taken. It SPENDS the claim too:
    /// there is nothing left to wait for, so holding it would be holding a ring
    /// slot for an answer that can never come.
    ///
    /// **IT IS NOT A `consumes` METHOD, AND THAT IS THE POLL SEMANTICS SPEAKING**
    /// (SL-16). Its two siblings on the obligation side — `reply` and
    /// `reply_wait` — consume on every path and say so in the effect slot. This
    /// one consumes on two paths out of three: `Ok(Some(n))` and
    /// `Err(PeerClosed)` both end the exchange, and `Ok(None)` is the answer that
    /// has not arrived yet and spends NOTHING. `consumes` cannot express
    /// consume-on-some-paths — it would kill the claim at the first empty poll,
    /// which is the one thing a poll loop must survive — so the receiver stays
    /// `&var self`, the disarm stays decided by the ANSWER, and a second resolve
    /// through a spent claim stays what it always was: the kernel's `BadHandle`,
    /// which `pipe-dead-claim` proves.
    public func resolve(&var self) -> Result<PipeMsg?, SosStatus> {
        resolve_into_frame(&var self)
    }

    /// **MINT A SIBLING of this claim, narrowed by a KEEP MASK** — the universal
    /// `MINT_OP`, on `PipeInlet.mint`'s exact terms.
    ///
    /// A sibling is a second NAME and therefore a second reference on the
    /// exchange's reply column, and single use survives it: the op consumes an
    /// ENTRY, and the exchange's own state is what allows exactly one successful
    /// resolve. The sibling that asks second is told `PeerClosed`, which is the
    /// same answer an abandoned peer gets and for the same reason — there is no
    /// reply left for it.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<PipeReply, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return PipeReply(handle: PipeReplyHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeReply.resolve`, confined to one small named helper
/// (design 136). The buffer's address never leaves it.
///
/// **THE DISARM IS DECIDED BY THE ANSWER, and that is this file's one departure
/// from the transfer-funnel contract** (`floor`'s block: disarm BEFORE the
/// syscall, because every failure of such a syscall is a fault). A resolve has a
/// third outcome the funnels do not — `Ok(None)`, which consumed nothing — so
/// disarming first would throw away a live claim on every poll. The rule is exact
/// and it is the kernel's: the caller's entry is consumed unless the answer is a
/// would-block.
func resolve_into_frame(claim: &var PipeReply) unsafe -> Result<PipeMsg?, SosStatus> {
    var rec = PipeMsgRecord(meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0,
                                              kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                            handles: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
                            bytes: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_pipe_reply_resolve(claim.handle,
                                     (&var rec) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                     pipe_msg_record_bytes() as UInt,
                                     (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    let got = try polled_value(raw, value) catch {
        claim.handle = PipeReplyHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        return error
    }
    guard let _ = got else { return None }
    claim.handle = PipeReplyHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    let answer: PipeMsg? = msg_of(&rec.meta, &rec.handles, &rec.bytes)
    return move answer
}

/// A handle to a PipeRequest — the SERVER's obligation to send ONE reply (spec
/// §2.1's ratified `PipeRequestHandle`; sawos design 14).
///
/// **IT IS WHAT A TAKE ANSWERS WITH BESIDE THE MESSAGE**, and it is the reason
/// §2.1 calls request/reply a primitive: the server does not have to know who
/// asked, or over which connection, or how to address an answer. It holds the
/// obligation, and the obligation knows.
///
/// **IT IS THE DELEGATION PRIMITIVE** (§2.1, ratified): `Transfer` is minted, so
/// a server may `give` this handle to a third party who replies STRAIGHT to the
/// original client — the filesystem forwarding a read to the flash driver, whose
/// bytes never transit the filesystem's address space. Single use is preserved
/// through the transfer, because it is a property of the exchange rather than of
/// whoever holds the handle.
///
/// **`NoCopy`, AND DROPPING IT WITHOUT REPLYING IS ALSO AN ANSWER**: the client's
/// `resolve` is told `PeerClosed`. Nothing is stranded, which is the peer-gone
/// doctrine reaching the last object in M4 that could have stranded anybody.
public struct PipeRequest {
    public(package) handle: PipeRequestHandle,
}

extension PipeRequest: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release. A discharged obligation holds the sentinel, so this is a
    /// no-op on it.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension PipeRequest {
    /// **SEND THE REPLY, AND DISCHARGE THE OBLIGATION.** Requires
    /// `PipeRequestRight.Reply`.
    ///
    /// **IT CONSUMES THIS CAPABILITY WHATEVER IT ANSWERS, AND THE TYPE SAYS SO**
    /// (SL-16, sawlang design 260). The declaration carries `consumes` and the
    /// call spells the move — `(move request).reply(body: &msg, len: n)` — so
    /// `request` is dead past that expression and a second reply through the same
    /// binding is a COMPILE error, not the `BadHandle` fault it used to be. The
    /// kernel's ledger still draws that fault for a raw-altitude caller holding a
    /// word; what changed is that a Saw caller can no longer get there.
    ///
    /// Nothing is disarmed and nothing is released: the consuming body stands in
    /// for this type's `deinit` body on this path, and the handle word it leaves
    /// behind is swept by a synthesized drop that does nothing.
    ///
    /// `len` may be ZERO: a reply that carries no data is still an answer, and it
    /// is how the TELL idiom's server side discharges
    /// (`let _ = (move request).reply(...)`).
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` means the client abandoned its claim and the
    /// reply went nowhere — §2.1's ratified answer, never a silent success. THE
    /// OBLIGATION IS DISCHARGED EITHER WAY, which is what makes ignoring it with
    /// `let _ =` correct rather than careless — and what makes `consumes`, which
    /// consumes on the Err path too, the exact effect for it.
    public func reply(&var self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                      len: UInt) consumes -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        var none = PipeHandles.none()
        return reply_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var none)
    }

    /// **THE SAME REPLY, CARRYING HANDLES** — §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)` in
    /// the answering direction (sawos design 18).
    ///
    /// The carrier is emptied on every path this method can return through: a
    /// reply is discharged whatever it answers, so there is no retry to keep
    /// handles for. **`Err(PeerClosed)` IS THE ONE THAT COSTS THEM** — the
    /// client abandoned its claim, nothing was staged, and the words the carrier
    /// held name entries this process still owns with no typed name left for
    /// them. It is the same trade `Process.give` makes at its own funnel, and it
    /// is stated rather than hidden.
    public func reply_with(&var self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                           handles: &var PipeHandles) consumes -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        reply_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var handles)
    }

    /// **SEND THE REPLY AND WAIT FOR THE NEXT THING — one trap** (sawos design
    /// 17; design 10 ruling 4's rider as amended Sep 1). Requires
    /// `PipeRequestRight.Reply` here and `WaiterRight.Wait` on `waiter`.
    ///
    /// **A REPLY WITH A BUILT-IN WAIT, WHICH IS WHY IT LIVES ON THE REQUEST**
    /// (user-ruled, Sep 1): the reply is the primary act and it is what consumes
    /// this capability, so the op belongs to the object being consumed and the
    /// wait is the fused tail. It halves the steady-state server loop — one trap
    /// per message instead of a `reply` and a `wait` — and it is the exact
    /// mirror of `PipeInlet.send` on the client side.
    ///
    /// **THE WAITER IS REQUIRED, AND THERE IS NO DEGENERATE FORM.** The FIRST
    /// turn of a server loop is `waiter.wait()`, which already exists;
    /// reply-without-wait is `reply()`, which already exists; and the recovery
    /// from a dead client is that same first turn. An optional waiter would
    /// change the return type on a runtime value, which is the shape
    /// `designs/010` ruling 4 and ruling 11(c) both refused.
    ///
    /// **THE ERROR NAMES ITS LEG, and `Wait(_)` MEANS THE REPLY LANDED**
    /// (user-ruled, Sep 1). `Err(Reply(s))` is a reply that did not land — the
    /// client abandoned its claim — and the op returned WITHOUT PARKING, so one
    /// dead client can never stall the loop; the caller recovers by waiting
    /// afresh. `Err(Wait(s))` is an exchange that completed and a wait that
    /// broke (`SosStatus.Revoked` is the one that exists today).
    ///
    /// **THE OBLIGATION IS DISCHARGED ON EVERY PATH**, §2.1's ratified sentence —
    /// which is why this is a `consumes` method exactly as `reply` is (SL-16,
    /// sawlang design 260). The call spells the move, the binding is dead after
    /// it, and reaching for it again is a COMPILE error. Both legs of the error
    /// consume: `Err(Reply(_))` did not deliver the reply and `Err(Wait(_))` did,
    /// and neither leaves an obligation anyone still holds.
    ///
    /// The loop it is written for:
    ///
    /// ```saw
    /// var wake = try waiter.wait() catch { return }
    /// while serving {
    ///     let request = decode(move wake)
    ///     let n = handle(&var out)
    ///     wake = try (move request).reply_wait(body: &out, len: n,
    ///                                          waiter: &waiter)
    ///           catch { ... }
    /// }
    /// ```
    public func reply_wait(&var self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                           waiter: &Waiter
    ) consumes -> Result<WaitResult, ReplyWaitError> {
        var none = PipeHandles.none()
        return reply_wait_body(self.handle, &body, len, waiter.handle, &var none)
    }

    /// **THE SAME FUSED REPLY, CARRYING HANDLES.** Everything
    /// `reply(handles:)` says about the carrier holds here too — the obligation
    /// is discharged on every path, so the handles go with it.
    public func reply_wait_with(&var self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                                len: UInt, waiter: &Waiter,
                                handles: &var PipeHandles
    ) consumes -> Result<WaitResult, ReplyWaitError> {
        reply_wait_body(self.handle, &body, len, waiter.handle, &var handles)
    }

    /// **MINT A SIBLING of this obligation, narrowed by a KEEP MASK.** Everything
    /// `PipeReply.mint` says holds here with `Reply` in `Resolve`'s place: one
    /// reply may be written however many names the obligation has, and the sibling
    /// that writes second is told `PeerClosed`.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<PipeRequest, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeRequest.reply`, confined to one small named helper
/// (design 136). The body's address never leaves it.
///
/// **THERE IS NO DISARM ANY MORE, AND NOTHING TAKES ITS PLACE** (SL-16). The
/// caller is a `consumes` method, so its body IS the wrapper's teardown on this
/// path: the custom `deinit` never runs, the handle word is swept by a
/// synthesized drop that does nothing, and the binding the caller moved from
/// releases nothing on any path. So this helper needs no wrapper at all — it
/// takes the word, exactly as `post_body`, `call_body` and `take_into` do, and
/// the file's three-argument shape is uniform again.
func reply_body(handle: PipeRequestHandle, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                len: UInt,
                handles: &var PipeHandles) unsafe -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
    var args = send_record(&body, len, &handles)
    let raw = sos_pipe_request_reply(handle,
                                     (&var args) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                     pipe_send_record_bytes() as UInt)
    handles.handed_over()
    return checked(raw)
}

/// **WHICH HALF OF A `reply_wait` FAILED** (sawos design 17; user-ruled Sep 1 as
/// the leg-tagged error).
///
/// A design-234 tier-2 domain enum: two cases that CARRY the leaf status rather
/// than restating its vocabulary, because the thing a caller needs to know is
/// not a new failure mode but WHICH OF TWO OPS produced a familiar one. There is
/// no `TimedOut`, no `Alloc` and no catch-all, because this domain cannot
/// produce them.
///
/// **`Wait` MEANS THE REPLY LANDED**, which is the whole reason the two cases
/// exist. A server loop reads them as two different instructions: `Reply` says
/// this client is gone, recover by waiting afresh; `Wait` says the exchange
/// completed and the WAITER is what broke, which is not something waiting again
/// can fix.
public enum ReplyWaitError {
    /// The reply did not land — the client abandoned its claim. The obligation
    /// is discharged all the same and NOTHING PARKED, so the loop's next act is
    /// its own `waiter.wait()`.
    case Reply(status: SosStatus),
    /// The reply LANDED and the wait that followed it did not.
    /// `SosStatus.Revoked` is the one this can be today: the Waiter's last
    /// handle went while this thread was parked on it.
    case Wait(status: SosStatus),
}

extension ReplyWaitError {
    /// Which leg, in words. The status renders itself.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Reply(_) -> "the reply did not land",
            case Wait(_) -> "the reply landed and the wait failed",
        }
    }
}

extension ReplyWaitError: Printable {
    /// Written out of `append` calls rather than interpolation, which is what
    /// keeps it alloc-free and therefore usable from a freestanding process
    /// (design 135/137). The status forwards to its own `format`.
    func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        try! into.append(self.describe())
        try! into.append(": ")
        match self {
            case Reply(status) -> { status.format(into: &var into) },
            case Wait(status) -> { status.format(into: &var into) },
        }
    }
}

extension ReplyWaitError: Error {}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeRequest.reply_wait`, confined to one small named
/// helper (design 136). Three addresses cross here and none of them leaves.
///
/// **NO DISARM, for `reply_body`'s exact reason** (SL-16): the caller is a
/// `consumes` method whose body stands in for the wrapper's teardown, so there is
/// no sentinel to write and no release to suppress. It takes the word.
///
/// **THE SMALL ARGUMENTS TRAVEL IN A RECORD AND THE BODY DOES NOT.** §5.7 gives
/// an op three argument registers and this one has four things to say, so the
/// waiter, the body's ADDRESS and its length ride the copy-in funnel
/// `Timer.arm` opened while the wait buffer keeps the register that is left.
/// Putting the body itself in the record would have made every call copy
/// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` in userspace before the trap, which is a cost the fused
/// path exists to avoid.
func reply_wait_body(handle: PipeRequestHandle, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                     len: UInt, waiter: WaiterHandle,
                     handles: &var PipeHandles) unsafe -> Result<WaitResult, ReplyWaitError> {
    var args = PipeReplyWaitRecord(waiter: waiter,
                                   msg: send_record(&body, len, &handles))
    var buf = WaitBuffer(header: [0; WAIT_HEADER_WORDS],
                         meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0, kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                         handles: [NO_HANDLE; WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS],
                         body: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    var leg: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_pipe_request_reply_wait(handle,
                                          (&var args) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                          pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes() as UInt,
                                          (&var buf.header) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                          (&var leg) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    handles.handed_over()
    try checked(raw) catch { return leg_error(error, leg) }
    return decode_wait(&buf)
}

/// Pair a status with the leg the kernel stamped beside it.
///
/// AN UNKNOWN LEG PANICS, on `decode_wait`'s exact terms: the kernel wrote it
/// out of the same `ReplyWaitLeg` declaration this function reads, so a value
/// that does not decode is a kernel/`sos` skew rather than data from an
/// untrusted source, and the never-hide rule says a bug the caller cannot act on
/// should stop the program where it happened.
func leg_error(status: SosStatus, leg: UInt) -> ReplyWaitError {
    guard let which = ReplyWaitLeg.from(raw: leg) else {
        panic("sos: kernel wrote reply-wait leg {}, which this build cannot name",
              leg)
    }
    match which {
        case Reply -> ReplyWaitError.Reply(status: status),
        case Wait -> ReplyWaitError.Wait(status: status),
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// WAITABILITY — the four pipe arms, and the consuming attach (spec §2.2's
// waitable list; `designs/010` rulings 8, 9 and 11; sawos design 15, M4 unit 3)
// =============================================================================
//
// **THE EIGHT OVERLOADS LIVE HERE AND NOT IN `sos.waiter`, ON DESIGN 8's OWN
// PRECEDENT.** `Waiter.add` reads its argument's handle field, so an overload
// must sit where the argument's type is declared; `sos.waiter` is BELOW this
// file (this file names `Waiter`, because the four overloads below take one), so
// an overload naming `PipeInlet` written there would import downward into its
// own importer — the cycle DF-232e does not diagnose. It is exactly the shape
// `Waiter.add(process:)` has had in `sos.system` since M3 unit 5.5, and since
// sawlang 0.2.0 extension lookup follows the facade's `public import`, so the
// split is invisible at the call site: `waiter.add(outlet:, key:)` reads as one
// more member of the one list.
//
// **THE 2x2 IS SPELLED BY THE VERB AND THE MODE** (ruling 11(c)): `add` borrows
// and `give` consumes, `AttachMode.Persistent` stays subscribed and
// `AttachMode.OneShot` detaches at delivery. Four combinations, two axes, and
// no `OneShotConsuming` anywhere — the axes are orthogonal, and consuming is an
// EFFECT change that a runtime flag may not make.

extension Waiter {
    /// **WATCH A CONNECTION'S SERVER END FOR TRAFFIC**: attach it under `key`,
    /// which a `wait()` hands back with an `OutletLevel` when a message is
    /// staged or the client end is gone (spec §2.2's readable arm). Requires
    /// `PipeOutletRight.Wait` there and `WaiterRight.Attach` here.
    ///
    /// **THIS IS WHAT TURNS UNIT 2's POLL LOOP INTO A PARK.** A server holding
    /// several outlets on one Waiter is §2.2's whole shape — the key says which
    /// connection woke it, and `take` is what it does next.
    ///
    /// `OutletLevel.PeerGone` is DRAIN-THEN-TERMINAL and arrives only once the
    /// ring is dry: everything the departed client staged is handed over first,
    /// which is design 230's close-drains-first rule and the same order
    /// `take`'s own `PeerClosed` follows. Nothing un-closes.
    public func add(&self, outlet: &PipeOutlet, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(outlet.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// **WATCH A CONNECTION'S CLIENT END FOR ROOM**: attach it under `key`,
    /// which a `wait()` hands back with an `InletLevel` while the staging ring
    /// has a free slot or the server end is gone (`designs/010` ruling 8).
    /// Requires `PipeInletRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// **IT EXISTS BECAUSE `post` CAN NEVER BLOCK.** Ruling 4 made post the one
    /// kernel primitive on the client side and gave it no park, so a refused
    /// poster needed something better than a poll loop — this is it, and it is
    /// the missing leg a caller's blocking `send` composition needs.
    ///
    /// A PLAIN LEVEL, NOT A TERMINAL ONE: consuming it clears nothing, and the
    /// level's end is the poster's own next post. So an attachment left
    /// `Persistent` on a connection with room reports ready every time it is
    /// asked, exactly as an Interrupt's emptiness does.
    public func add(&self, inlet: &PipeInlet, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(inlet.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// **WATCH A CLAIM FOR ITS ANSWER**: attach it under `key`, which a `wait()`
    /// hands back with a `ReplyDelivery` — THE REPLY BYTES THEMSELVES — when the
    /// server answers, or `PeerClosed` when it never will. Requires
    /// `PipeReplyRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// **THE DELIVERY CARRIES THE PAYLOAD** (`designs/010` ruling 11(a)), which
    /// is what makes this attach different in kind from the three beside it: a
    /// client that waits this way never calls `resolve` at all, because the
    /// answer arrived in the wait record. The multiplexed client is post ->
    /// attach -> wait, three traps, however many requests are outstanding.
    ///
    /// **A DELIVERY IS INHERENTLY CONSUMING HERE, WHATEVER `mode` SAYS** (ruling
    /// 11(b)): a claim is a ONE-SHOT, the answer is spent by the copy-out, and a
    /// persistent subscription to something that has happened for the last time
    /// would be a promise nothing can keep. So the attachment ends at the
    /// delivery and `mode` is inert on this kind — stated rather than refused,
    /// because the parameter is uniform across the family and a per-kind
    /// exception would be a rule to remember.
    ///
    /// THE HANDLE STAYS YOURS with `add`. What a later `resolve` on it answers
    /// is `PeerClosed`: the exchange was discharged by the delivery, and there
    /// is no second copy of an answer. Use `give` when the claim is genuinely
    /// finished with — it is, on the success path, which is why the composed
    /// `send` is the one caller that keeps it.
    public func add(&self, reply: &PipeReply, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(reply.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// **WATCH AN OBLIGATION FOR ITS CANCELLATION**: attach it under `key`,
    /// which a `wait()` hands back with `WaitPayload.Abandoned` once the client
    /// has dropped the claim behind it (spec §2.1's ratified early notice).
    /// Requires `PipeRequestRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// **ABANDONMENT IS INFORMATION, NOT AN IMPERATIVE** (§2.1). This is the
    /// opt-in early notice the ratified text names, and what a server does with
    /// it is the protocol's decision: a cancellable read stops working, a
    /// tell-style message ignores it entirely. Without the attach, a server
    /// learns the same fact when its `reply` answers `PeerClosed` — later, and
    /// after the work.
    ///
    /// TERMINAL, on the Process arm's exact terms: a reply column that reached
    /// zero can never rise, so a server attaching AFTER the client gave up is
    /// told at once and nothing un-abandons.
    public func add(&self, request: &PipeRequest, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(request.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// **HAND A CLAIM TO THIS WAITER, AND STOP HOLDING IT** (`designs/010`
    /// ruling 11(c); the pattern that motivated the whole ruling).
    ///
    /// A client that attaches its claim and takes the answer out of the wait
    /// record has no use for the handle afterwards — holding it is pure
    /// ceremony, and a table row. `give` says so: the wrapper goes in by MOVE,
    /// the kernel releases the entry as part of the attach, and the ATTACHMENT
    /// owns the exchange from there. So a multiplexer holds ZERO reply handles
    /// however many requests are outstanding, and its `MAX_HANDLES` stops being
    /// the bound on its in-flight depth (design 14 finding 5, answered).
    ///
    /// **AND HANGING UP IS DESTROYING THE SUBSCRIPTION**: with no handle left,
    /// `remove(key)` drops the last reference, which is §2.1's cancellation
    /// primitive reached through the Waiter. That is the abandonment re-wording
    /// this unit lands — a handle drop with a live attachment is not
    /// abandonment, because the subscription is the interested party.
    ///
    /// Everything `Waiter.give(event:)` says about the verb, the gate and the
    /// object-handed-back refusal holds word for word.
    public func give(&self, reply: PipeReply, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, PipeReply)> {
        var owned = move reply
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeReplyHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, PipeReply) = (error, PipeReply(handle: PipeReplyHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Hand an OBLIGATION to this Waiter — the cancellation watcher that owns
    /// what it watches. Everything `give(reply:)` says holds, with one
    /// consequence worth naming: the obligation is now the attachment's, so
    /// `remove(key)` DISCHARGES it and the client's `resolve` answers
    /// `PeerClosed`. A server hangs up on a request by unsubscribing from it.
    public func give(&self, request: PipeRequest, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, PipeRequest)> {
        var owned = move request
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeRequestHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, PipeRequest) = (error, PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Hand a connection's SERVER END to this Waiter. Everything
    /// `give(reply:)` says holds — and this is the half `designs/010` ruling
    /// 11(d) builds the completion-queue server on in unit 4, where the delivery
    /// becomes the TAKE. Today it is the plainer thing: a subscription that owns
    /// its end, so `remove(key)` drops the server end to zero and the
    /// connection's clients answer `PeerClosed`.
    public func give(&self, outlet: PipeOutlet, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, PipeOutlet)> {
        var owned = move outlet
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeOutletHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, PipeOutlet) = (error, PipeOutlet(handle: PipeOutletHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Hand a connection's CLIENT END to this Waiter. The mirror, and the least
    /// useful of the eight — an end nobody holds cannot be posted through — so
    /// it exists for uniformity and for the one shape that wants it: a watcher
    /// that only cares whether the far side is still there.
    public func give(&self, inlet: PipeInlet, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, PipeInlet)> {
        var owned = move inlet
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeInletHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, PipeInlet) = (error, PipeInlet(handle: PipeInletHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }
}
